WoW Forever Talent Value Calculator
Implementation Plan

Document status: Accepted — ready for implementation
Target: WoW Forever, using its own client data and Forever-specific mechanics
Version 1 scope: Character stat calculator with architecture designed for future combat simulation

1. Project objective

Build a web application that calculates character stats and evaluates the relative value of talents in WoW Forever.

The application will allow users to:

Select a character class and race.
Select a character level from 1 to 60.
Interact with the character's talent trees.
View character-pane stats in a structured table.
Edit manual stat adjustments.
See supported talent effects update the stat table immediately.
Compare character builds and quantify their stat differences.
Eventually estimate talent value in terms of DPS, healing, and survivability.

The application must use Forever-specific data rather than assuming original Vanilla WoW or Classic Era mechanics are authoritative.

2. Design principles
Data-driven: Game data must be separate from application logic.
Versioned: Every dataset must identify its source client build and revision.
Traceable: Calculated values should be explainable through their inputs and modifiers.
Incremental: Unsupported mechanics must be marked explicitly rather than guessed.
Testable: Calculation rules must be testable independently of the UI.
Extensible: The stat engine must support a future combat simulator without requiring a rewrite.
Reproducible: The same configuration and data revision must produce the same results.
Updatable: New client builds must be ingestible through a repeatable, validated pipeline, with changes reviewable before activation and reversible after it.
3. Scope of version 1
3.1 Character configuration
Nine classes, subject to validation against the target build.
All supported race variants and class/race combinations.
Character levels 1–60.
Race- and class-dependent starting stats.
Level-dependent stat growth.
Racial passive modifiers.
Talent-tree selection and rank management.
3.2 Character stat table

The initial stat schema will cover the stats actually present in the target game's character pane, with additional derived stats where reliable data is available.

Candidate attributes:

Strength
Agility
Stamina
Intellect
Spirit
Health and Mana
Armor
Attack Power
Ranged Attack Power
Spell Power and healing power, where supported
Hit and critical strike
Defense, dodge, parry, and block
Other confirmed stats relevant to the target build

This is a candidate list, not a claim that every stat exists or is displayed by WoW Forever.

Each stat should distinguish:

Base value
Manual adjustment
Racial modifier
Talent modifier
Other modifiers, added in later phases
Final calculated value
Verification status and provenance

Manual adjustments must never silently overwrite base stats.

3.3 Talent trees
Display the selected class's talent trees.
Support clicking talents to allocate and remove ranks.
Enforce maximum ranks, prerequisites, and point budgets.
Recalculate supported stat effects immediately.
Support build reset and comparison.
Identify unsupported or unverified effects.
3.4 Relative talent value

Version 1 will focus on stat deltas:

Delta = Stat with talent - Stat without talent

Later versions may calculate normalised stat values and combat performance under explicit assumptions.

A universal talent score will not be introduced until its assumptions and weighting methodology are defined.
3.5 Data updates

Version 1 will include a one-click update flow in the UI:

Detect when a newer client build than the active dataset is available.
Fetch and process the new build's data using the same pipeline used for the initial dataset.
Show a summary of what changed before applying it.
Apply the update on user confirmation, with no manual file handling.
Allow rollback to the previous dataset.
Keep every previously installed dataset available for build-to-build comparison.

The update flow must never apply a dataset that fails validation.

